1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,707
Is deep learning really as new as it feels?

2
00:00:08,707 --> 00:00:14,481
Back in nineteen fifty, Turing was already picturing machines that learn their way to intelligence.

3
00:00:14,481 --> 00:00:17,806
In nineteen fifty-seven, Rosenblatt built the perceptron,

4
00:00:17,806 --> 00:00:23,600
and around the same time the least mean squares rule showed up in adaptive signal processing.

5
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:29,242
Then in nineteen sixty-nine, Minsky and Papert wrote a whole book about what these networks can't do.

6
00:00:29,242 --> 00:00:33,097
In nineteen eighty-six, backpropagation made deep networks trainable,

7
00:00:33,097 --> 00:00:37,666
and by nineteen eighty-nine a network called LeNet was reading handwriting.

8
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:43,634
After that the field drifted toward probabilistic and convex methods, with mostly shallow models.

9
00:00:43,634 --> 00:00:47,386
Around two thousand six, deep networks started creeping back.

10
00:00:47,386 --> 00:00:52,600
And in twenty twelve, Krizhevsky's AlexNet beat every other method on ImageNet.

11
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,704
From the perceptron to AlexNet is fifty-five years.

12
00:00:55,704 --> 00:01:02,833
So learned circuits are an old idea, and what's new is that they finally work this well.

13
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:06,053
So what was the complaint back in nineteen sixty-nine?

14
00:01:06,053 --> 00:01:11,360
Take four points labeled by exclusive or, which is plus one when exactly one input is on.

15
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:13,447
One element can only draw one line.

16
00:01:13,447 --> 00:01:20,066
Try any line you like, and you can get three of the points right, but never all four.

17
00:01:20,066 --> 00:01:23,328
But stack two elements and the problem goes away.

18
00:01:23,328 --> 00:01:30,119
Put two ramps on the sum of the inputs, where the second one only switches on once the sum passes one.

19
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:33,315
Then take the first ramp minus twice the second.

20
00:01:33,315 --> 00:01:39,866
Run the four rows through, and out comes zero, zero, one, one, which is exactly exclusive or.

21
00:01:39,866 --> 00:01:42,591
So one element can't do it, but a stack can.

22
00:01:42,591 --> 00:01:49,733
And making stacks trainable is exactly what backpropagation did in nineteen eighty-six.

23
00:01:49,733 --> 00:01:55,129
Now for an awkward question, which is whether deep learning is engineering or alchemy.

24
00:01:55,129 --> 00:01:58,079
There's a famous xkcd comic about exactly this.

25
00:01:58,079 --> 00:02:01,844
Its machine learning system is a big pile of linear algebra,

26
00:02:01,844 --> 00:02:06,466
where you pour data in on one side and collect answers on the other.

27
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:10,995
And if the answers come out wrong, you just stir the pile until they start looking right.

28
00:02:10,995 --> 00:02:15,100
That's what alchemy looks like.

29
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:18,325
So what would engineering look like instead of stirring?

30
00:02:18,325 --> 00:02:24,966
Take a single knob, the learning rate, and run gradient descent on a simple bowl, starting from w equals one.

31
00:02:24,966 --> 00:02:27,207
Here are four settings of that knob.

32
00:02:27,207 --> 00:02:31,254
At zero point one and at zero point four, w settles down to zero.

33
00:02:31,254 --> 00:02:37,200
At zero point five it bounces back and forth forever, and at zero point six it blows up.

34
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:41,274
The reason is that each step multiplies w by one minus four eta,

35
00:02:41,274 --> 00:02:45,221
and that factor only shrinks w while eta stays under one half.

36
00:02:45,221 --> 00:02:47,322
That's engineering, not stirring.

37
00:02:47,322 --> 00:02:54,333
It's a knob with a reason behind it, so you know where it works and where it breaks.

38
00:02:54,333 --> 00:03:00,176
This course sits on top of three earlier ones, and they line up with the three parts of the definition.

39
00:03:00,176 --> 00:03:05,112
Machine learning brings the patterns, optimization brings the circuits and their knobs,

40
00:03:05,112 --> 00:03:08,933
and probability tells us about new data.

41
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:10,804
So that's the plan for the course.

42
00:03:10,804 --> 00:03:17,066
We'll design deep networks, look inside them, and understand why they work.

43
00:03:17,066 --> 00:03:23,177
There's one last thing to read alongside this, which is Terence Tao's essay, Mathematics in the Age of AI.

44
00:03:23,177 --> 00:03:27,500
He sets aside what the tools can do, and asks what a field is actually for.

45
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:32,300
That's a question worth asking of deep learning too.

